  "output": "Both layers inspected: `web/` is a fully-built Next.js/Drizzle console; `engine/` is currently all `raise NotImplementedError` stubs (Phase 2/3 per AGENTS.md's own build order), so I'm flagging engine items as design risks to avoid when you implement them, not existing bugs.\n\n## N+1 query patterns\n\n**Not present in the read paths I checked** — `getQueuePage` (`web/lib/server/conflicts.ts:172`) is explicitly written to avoid it: rows/counts/total run in `Promise.all`, and child counts for the whole page come from one grouped `countChildrenByParent` call rather than per-row queries. `export.ts`'s `loadOrphans` does the same. This is a genuine strength worth preserving as the queue grows.\n\nOne real inefficiency, though — `getQueueCounts` (`conflicts.ts:249`) scans `conflicts JOIN resolutions` **twice** with nearly the same predicate:\n\n```ts\n// conflicts.ts:250-264 — two full scans of the same join\nconst [byStatus, bySeverity] = await Promise.all([\n  getDb().select({ status: resolutions.status, total: count() })...groupBy(resolutions.status),\n  getDb().select({ severity: conflicts.severity, total: count() })...groupBy(conflicts.severity),\n]);\n```\nCombine into one query using conditional aggregation:\n```ts\nconst [rows] = await getDb()\n  .select({\n    status: resolutions.status,\n    severity: conflicts.severity,\n    total: count(),\n  })\n  .from(conflicts)\n  .leftJoin(resolutions, eq(resolutions.conflictId, conflicts.id))\n  .where(and(eq(conflicts.runId, runId), ...filters(query, \"status_and_severity\")))\n  .groupBy(resolutions.status, conflicts.severity);\n// then reduce `rows` into both breakdowns client-side\n```\n\n**Engine risk (Phase 2/3, not yet built):** `AGENTS.md` already names the trap in `blocking.py`'s docstring — \"NEVER compare all pairs.\" The one not called out: when `resolve()` in `agent/graph.py` is implemented, don't `await` the Claude call inside a per-conflict `for` loop. That's an N+1 against the Anthropic API. Batch it — either the Batch API (already mandated for `eval/` sweeps) or `asyncio.gather` with a concurrency cap for the live path.\n\n## Unnecessary re-renders in React\n\n**The real one:** none of `ConflictDiffRow`, `ConflictDenseTable`'s row, or `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow` are wrapped in `React.memo`. `selectedIndex` lives in the parent `ConflictQueue` (`web/components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:46`), so every `j`/`k` keypress re-renders **all** rows in the list, not just the previously-selected and newly-selected ones. On a 400-row escalation queue (the exact scenario the keyboard-nav feature targets in AGENTS.md) that's 400 component trees re-diffed per arrow key.\n\nIt's compounded by fresh closures per row on every render:\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx:174-187 — new closures every render, blocks memoization\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}                       // new fn\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}                              // new fn\n    onOverrideOpenChange={(open) => setOverrideOpen(row.conflictId, open)} // new fn\n    ...\n```\nFix — memoize the row and give it stable, conflictId-keyed callbacks instead of per-row bound closures:\n```tsx\nconst handleSelect = useCallback((index: number) => setSelectedIndex(index), []);\nconst handleOverrideOpenChange = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string, open: boolean) => setOverrideOpen(open ? conflictId : null),\n  [],\n);\n\n// ConflictDiffRow.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ row, index, onSelect, ... }) {\n  ...\n}, (prev, next) =>\n  prev.row === next.row &&\n  prev.selected === next.selected &&\n  prev.pending === next.pending &&\n  prev.decidedStatus === next.decidedStatus &&\n  prev.overrideOpen === next.overrideOpen\n);\n```\n`row` objects come straight from a server render each time (new reference per page load anyway), so the memo comparator matters most for the `selected`/`pending`/`overrideOpen` fields that change on interaction — those are exactly what currently forces every row to re-render together.\n\n**Minor:** `staggerIndex` in `conflict-queue.tsx:137` is wrapped in `useMemo` for a one-line `Math.min` — the memoization overhead exceeds the computation it's saving. Harmless, just pointless:\n```tsx\nconst staggerIndex = useMemo(() => (index: number) => Math.min(index, 24), []); // drop the useMemo\nconst staggerIndex = (index: number) => Math.min(index, 24);\n```\n\n## Caching opportunities\n\n**Rulesets are versioned and immutable by design** (AGENTS.md: \"never edited in place\") — this is the single best caching target in the codebase and it isn't exploited. `getRuleset(rulesetId)` (called from `loadQueue`, `conflicts.ts:226`) hits Postgres on every queue page load even though a given `rulesetId`'s rows can never change:\n```ts\nimport { unstable_cache } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const getRuleset = unstable_cache(\n  async (rulesetId: string) => { /* existing query */ },\n  [\"ruleset\"],\n  { revalidate: false }, // immutable — a rulesetId's content never changes once created\n);\n```\n\n**`sources` and the active ruleset are re-fetched independently on every page that needs them** (`rules/page.tsx`, `runs/new/page.tsx`, `get-started/page.tsx`, `sources/page.tsx` each call `listSources()` fresh). These change rarely (a handful of writes total in the app's lifetime). Wrap in React's request-scoped `cache()` at minimum to dedupe within a render pass, and consider a short `unstable_cache` with tag-based invalidation on writes:\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { unstable_cache, revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async () => { /* existing query */ },\n  [\"sources\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n\n// in createSource, after the insert:\nrevalidateTag(\"sources\");\n```\n\n**`countSourcesBySide()`** (`sources.ts:24`) fetches every column — including the `config` jsonb blob — for every source, just to count by `kind`:\n```ts\n// current: full row fetch to count\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return { crm: all.filter(...).length, ... };\n}\n```\nPush the count into SQL instead:\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, total: count() })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // reduce rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic }\n}\n```\n\n**Engine (per AGENTS.md, not yet implemented):** the doc already mandates prompt caching on the ruleset block for the resolution agent. Worth reinforcing with the actual shape, since it's easy to get wrong with the Anthropic SDK — the `cache_control` breakpoint has to be on the last block of the *stable* prefix:\n```python\nmessages = [{\n    \"role\": \"user\",\n    \"content\": [\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ruleset_block, \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": conflict_specific_prompt},\n    ],\n}]\n```\n\n## Memory leaks\n\n**None found in the current `web/` code** — worth confirming since these are the classic Next.js foot-guns and this codebase avoids them:\n- DB connection is cached on `globalThis` (`lib/db/index.ts:22`) specifically to survive HMR reloads in dev — correct pattern.\n- `RunLive`'s poll (`run-live.tsx`) stops via TanStack Query's `refetchInterval` returning `false` on terminal status, and `Elapsed`'s `setInterval` is cleared in its effect's cleanup.\n- `ConflictQueue`'s `window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", ...)` is removed in the effect cleanup.\n\n**Engine risk (Phase 2, not yet built) — this is the one to actively design around:** `blocking.py` will load a `sentence-transformers` model and build a FAISS index. If `block()` (currently `raise NotImplementedError`) loads the model inside the function body rather than once at module import, every run reloads a multi-hundred-MB model into memory — that's both a redundant-computation cost per run and, under FastAPI's `BackgroundTasks` (which reuses the same process across requests, `main.py:38`), a leak: each run's model/index instance stays referenced somewhere or gets GC'd only after a full run, causing memory to saw up and down instead of staying flat. Load once, module-level:\n```python\n# pipeline/blocking.py\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=1)\ndef _embedder() -> SentenceTransformer:\n    return SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")  # loaded once per process, not per run\n```\nRelated: `execute_run` (`main.py:48`) runs as a synchronous `BackgroundTasks` callback in the same event loop as `/runs/{run_id}/status`. If the pipeline's pandas/FAISS work is CPU-bound and synchronous, it will block that event loop and the polling endpoint will stall for the duration of the run — worth routing through `run_in_executor` or a separate worker process once Phase 1 lands.\n\n## Redundant computations\n\n**`RulesetEditor`** (`web/components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:61-179`) recomputes the evaluation order on every render and then does an `O(n)` `indexOf` lookup per row inside the map — `O(n²)` per render for what's a small, static list, but it's the pattern to flag before someone reuses it on a bigger list:\n```tsx\n// current — recomputed every render, indexOf per row\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);          // O(n) lookup\n  return <RuleRow position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1} ... />;  // another O(n) lookup\n})}\n```\n```tsx\nconst ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\nconst orderedWithMeta = useMemo(\n  () => ordered.map((rule, position) => ({\n    rule,\n    position: position + 1,\n    index: rules.indexOf(rule), // still O(n) but computed once, not twice\n  })),\n  [ordered, rules],\n);\n```\nCleaner fix: carry the original index through `orderRulesByEvaluation` itself instead of recovering it via `indexOf` afterward (it already computes `{ rule, index }` internally at `types/rules.ts:90` and throws the index away).\n\n**Duplicated logic, not perf-critical but worth a mention:** `sourceName()` is defined identically in both `conflicts.ts:390` and `export.ts:263` — same lookup, same fallback string, two copies to keep in sync. Worth hoisting into `lib/server/runs.ts` next to `RunDetail`.\n\n---\n\n**Priority if you want to act on this now:** the row-memoization fix (re-render section) is the highest-leverage one — it's the only item that scales badly with real usage (a 400-conflict queue, which is the app's own stated design target), while everything else is either already handled well or a small/forward-looking optimization.\n",
